  related={['strategy', 'howToWin', 'counting', 'spain']}
>
  <div slot="top">
    <div class="tldr"><p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> you win by finishing closer to 21 than the dealer without going over. Face cards are worth 10, aces 1 or 11, everything else its number. You choose whether to hit or stand; the dealer doesn’t choose — they hit until 17.</p></div>
  </div>

  <nav class="toc" aria-label="In this guide">
    <b>In this guide</b>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#goal">The goal</a></li>
      <li><a href="#values">Card values</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hand">How a hand plays out</a></li>
      <li><a href="#options">Your options: hit, stand, double, split, surrender</a></li>
      <li><a href="#payouts">Payouts</a></li>
      <li><a href="#insurance">Insurance (and why to skip it)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#next">Next step: basic strategy</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="goal">The goal</h2>
  <p>In blackjack you don’t play against the other players — you play <strong>against the dealer</strong>. You win a hand if your total is closer to 21 than theirs without going over, or if they bust and you don’t.</p>
  <p>Go over 21 and you bust: you lose immediately, even if the dealer busts afterwards. Because you act first, that one rule is where the house gets its edge.</p>

  <h2 id="values">Card values</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Card</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>2 to 9</td><td>Face value</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10, J, Q, K</td><td>10</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ace</td><td>1 or 11, whichever helps you</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Suits don’t matter. With four ten-value ranks, almost a third of the deck is worth 10 — which explains a lot of basic strategy.</p>
  <p>A hand with an ace counted as 11 is <strong>soft</strong>: ace-6 is “soft 17”. You can hit it safely, because if the next card would bust you the ace becomes 1. Every other hand is <strong>hard</strong>.</p>
  <p>A <strong>blackjack</strong> (or “natural”) is an ace plus a ten-value card as your first two cards. It’s the best hand and beats any other 21.</p>

  <h2 id="hand">How a hand plays out</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Bets.</strong> Everyone places a bet before the deal.</li>
    <li><strong>Deal.</strong> You get two cards face up. The dealer gets one face up and, in American games, one face down (the “hole card”).</li>
    <li><strong>Blackjacks.</strong> If the dealer shows an ace or ten, they peek at the hole card; with a blackjack, the hand ends there. If you have blackjack and the dealer doesn’t, you’re paid at once.</li>
    <li><strong>Your turn.</strong> Hit, stand, double, split or surrender. You can hit as many times as you like until you stand or bust.</li>
    <li><strong>Dealer’s turn.</strong> The dealer reveals the hole card and hits on 16 or less, stands on 17 or more.</li>
    <li><strong>Settle.</strong> Totals are compared and bets paid.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="options">Your options</h2>
  <h3>Hit</h3>
  <p>Take another card. Repeat until you stand or bust.</p>
  <h3>Stand</h3>
  <p>Keep your total and end your turn.</p>
  <h3>Double down</h3>
  <p>Double your bet in exchange for <strong>exactly one more card</strong>. It pays off with a strong starting total (9, 10 or 11) against a weak dealer card.</p>
  <h3>Split</h3>
  <p>If your first two cards match, separate them into two hands with an equal bet on the second. Always split aces and eights; never split tens or fives.</p>
  <h3>Surrender</h3>
  <p>Where it’s offered, give up the hand right after the deal and get half your bet back. Worth it only in a few very bad spots, like 16 against a dealer 10.</p>

  <h2 id="payouts">Payouts</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Pays</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Win</td><td>1 to 1 ($10 bet wins $10)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Blackjack</td><td>3 to 2 ($10 bet wins $15)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Push</td><td>Bet returned</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Insurance</td><td>2 to 1 on the insurance bet</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Watch out for 6:5 tables.</strong> When blackjack pays 6 to 5 instead of 3 to 2, the house edge goes up by about 1.4 percentage points — worse than any other single rule.</p></div>

  <h2 id="insurance">Insurance (and why to skip it)</h2>
  <p>When the dealer shows an ace you’ll be offered <strong>insurance</strong>: a side bet of up to half your wager that the dealer has blackjack. It pays 2 to 1.</p>
  <p>It sounds like protection, but it’s just another bet — and a bad one. In a six-deck game the chance of a ten under the ace is about 31%, while insurance needs more than 33% just to break even. The house edge on it is around 7%. Basic strategy says <strong>never take it</strong> — including “even money” when you hold blackjack.</p>

  <h2 id="next">Next step: basic strategy</h2>
  <p>Knowing the rules is half the job. The other half is knowing what to do in each spot: hit 12 against a 3? Double 11 against a 10? Split nines against a 7? Every combination has a mathematically correct answer, and together they form <a href={routes.strategy.en}>basic strategy</a>.</p>
  <p>Playing on instinct, most people give the house 2% or more. With basic strategy, the house edge drops to around 0.5%. It won’t make you a winner, but it’s the difference between playing well and playing blind.</p>
</Guide>
